Z||000002||Will||02-04-2001||10:53 AM||||I think it would be possible to use F-body front cartridge hubs in the front, as Ray B said, and Cadillac front hubs in the rear, but that would take a good deal of machining and engineering. You might be able to use the Cadillac hub carrier and brakes in the rear, but you'd need to have axles made up because the splines in the Caddy hub are much larger than the ones on the Fiero axle. The front end would take a lot of machining and figuring out. You might be able to adapt a steering knuckle from another car (F-body? Do they have double wishbone or struts in front?). Normally I would say be wary of altering suspension geometry, but it would be hard to make the 84-87 front geometry any worse...||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||02-04-2001||04:40 PM||||Ciera/6000 front uprights are a direct bolt in for the rear of the Fiero, except that they accept the larger Cadillac bearing/hub assemblies with a 4.5" pattern. If the Fiero has a manual trans, the outer CVs from the Ciera/6000 will fit the Fiero axle shafts and the larger hub splines. Brakes would need to be mdoified to fit. There is no way to re-drill the Fiero rear hubs because there are large holes between the existing studs.<P>For the front, you may be able to re-drill. I haven't investigated the front yet.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||03-15-2001||07:34 PM||||needed to drag this post up because i think i'm going to try this. i'm going to start looking for the pieces rockcrawl mentions but i'd like to find more detailed instructions if there are any out there. if anyone knows any web pages pertaining to this 4.5" bolt pattern swap i would appreciate an address.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||03-15-2001||10:01 PM||||Mind you I have a Northstar tranny but the Cadillac Allante hub and Pontiac 6000 spindle will fit.  The trouble as was mentioned is the axles.  It can be done but you loose the e-brake.  I've got pictures on my site of how it all looks <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||03-15-2001||10:15 PM||||Rear:<BR> You need to use the 6000 uprights and Cadillac Allante bearing assemblies. Outer CV joints from a Cadillac Allante (lots of other cars fit too) with fiero manual axles and inner joints. If you've got an auto trans, you'll need to do more research. I've got all the pieces here to make it work, but I don't know what they're all from. It can be done using standard GM parts<P>Front:<BR> Can't help you there.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||03-16-2001||09:48 AM||||Lots less work but way more expensive unless you know a machinist. Get him to make you all four new rotors with the bolt pattern you want. Wouldnt be able to make vented rotors, but solid wouldnt be hard at all. I had it done for a stock car I raced.<P><BR>just had a thought. wonder if you could call a rotor manufacturer like Borg-Warner and have them make make you a set with the holes you want or send you a set of blank rotors so you could do it yourself???? just a thought.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Philphine||03-16-2001||08:02 PM||||i may be about to show that i don't need to be trying this but, what are uprights? do they have any other name? jay, i looked at your site but it just describes the brake system. the pictures are of the engine. i still feel i could do this, in the rear any way, if i could get a clear picture of what i need and a procedure. i'll keep looking for info and start searching for parts.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||03-17-2001||12:07 AM||||Uprights, in the rear, are also called hub carriers. Take you wheel off and look at your rear suspension. The upright or hub carrier is the cast iron piece that bolts to the strut, the ball joint, the toe link, and to which the brake caliper is bolted. The front is similar, except that there is a ball joint at the top of the front one, rather than a strut.<P>Anyway, Pontiac 6000 front hub carriers, hubs, brake rotors, brakes, and outer CV joints can be directly transplanted into the Fiero. You might use Cadillac hubs, but there is no reason to, unless you can use a hub with an ABS wheel speed sensor.<BR>I'm not entirely sure exactly how, but P6000 outer CV joints can work with Fiero axles. I'm not sure if the splines are the same and the entire joint gets transplanted, or if just the the P6000 outer socket gets used with the Fiero inner guts.<P>I'm looking into such a transplant for the front. Both B-body and C4 Corvette steering knuckles are three inches taller than the Fiero spindles. I have yet to measure a C5, but I don't hold much hope for that.<BR>I haven't analysed the Fiero geometry yet to see how increasing the upright height that much will affect the suspension geometry.<P>What wheels are you going to use when you do change your bolt circle?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||03-17-2001||12:10 AM||||Oh yeah, I almost forgot: a machinist might be able to make you a new set of front hubs that would take a separate rotor (like an F-body rotor) and have the 5x115 circle. I don't knoe what he'd want to do about the bearing races inside, though.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 03-17-2001).]||||reg||1
